# Copy to .env.local (it is gitignored) and fill in. eve loads .env and .env.local.
#
# Variable NAMES only. Never commit a value. .env.local never ships: the deployment reads its OWN
# environment. The full setup path, including which variables the deployment needs and how to copy
# each one without printing it, is the generated reference page:
# https://github.com/open-everywhere/open-everywhere/blob/main/docs/deploy/setup-steps.md
# (README.md § "Set it up" is the one-screen version.) Two routes are supported: Vercel and Docker.

# --- Models: the Vercel AI Gateway ---
# A deployed VERCEL project authenticates to the gateway with OIDC and needs no key. Everything
# else does: a local run, and the DOCKER route, where there is no OIDC. Running a different model
# backend instead is an adapter swap in app code, not an env setting (docs/extending/adapter-model.md).
# Create a key at: Vercel dashboard -> AI Gateway -> API Keys.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# --- The operational store, memory and the credential store ---
# A DEPLOYMENT needs ONE value here: DATABASE_URL. On Vercel a function's disk is ephemeral, and a
# replaced Docker container starts from an empty volume, so without it core throws at the first
# store or DM-memory use. On the Vercel route add Neon from the Marketplace
# (`npx vercel install neon`, or project -> Storage -> Neon -> Connect): it sets DATABASE_URL on the
# project. Copy the same value here (`npx oe env set DATABASE_URL`, in your own terminal) so the operator tools (`npx oe secret`, the ops server's
# `secret_set`) write the store the deployment reads. A local run needs nothing: it uses an embedded
# database under .open-everywhere-data/.
DATABASE_URL=
# 🔴 NOT REQUIRED (OPE-2230). A present DATABASE_URL resolves BOTH stores to postgres on its own, so
# there is no selector to set and nothing to push to the deployment. These two are an OVERRIDE only
# — and a selector pointing AWAY from postgres is the one thing that breaks the resolution.
# OE_OPERATIONAL_PROVIDER=postgres
# OE_MEMORY_PROVIDER=postgres
# A base64-encoded 32-byte AES-256 key that encrypts stored credentials. The deployment and the
# operator tools need the same value. This command appends a fresh one to .env.local without printing it:
#   node -e "console.log('OE_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=' + require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))" >> .env.local
# OE_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=   (append it with the command above; do not type it here)
# Set this only for a custom domain. On Vercel the deployment reads its production host itself.
# OE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://agent.example.com
#
# After DATABASE_URL and the key are set, `npx oe secret set <NAME>` (value on stdin) stores a secret
# whose credential names `broker: "db"` in the encrypted store. The Slack variables and
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET below are read from the ENVIRONMENT only, so they stay environment variables.

# --- Telemetry (https://github.com/open-everywhere/open-everywhere/blob/main/docs/deploy/telemetry.md) ---
# The deployed app sends ANONYMOUS USAGE TELEMETRY, ON BY DEFAULT: event names, enums, coarse
# buckets, public model ids and the app version, with a random per-install id (service.instance.id).
# Never message or memory content, org names, workspace, channel, user or session ids, or env
# values. The page above lists every event. To turn it
# off, set this ON THE DEPLOYMENT (the Vercel project or the container's environment): a value here
# only reaches a local run, because .env.local never ships. DO_NOT_TRACK=1 does the same.
# OE_TELEMETRY=off

# --- Slack (https://github.com/open-everywhere/open-everywhere/blob/main/docs/deploy/surfaces/slack.md) ---
# Slack app -> OAuth & Permissions -> Bot User OAuth Token (xoxb-…).
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
# Slack app -> Basic Information -> Signing Secret. Verifies every inbound event.
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# --- GitHub (https://github.com/open-everywhere/open-everywhere/blob/main/docs/deploy/surfaces/github.md) ---
# The GitHub App that receives webhooks and replies as the app.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# Verifies inbound deliveries. If it is unset, the route refuses every delivery.
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Notion (https://github.com/open-everywhere/open-everywhere/blob/main/docs/deploy/surfaces/notion.md) ---
# Notion shows the webhook verification token once, at subscription time. Store it here or with
# `npx oe secret set NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET` (the stored value wins over this variable).
# NOTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
